# Cross scores

A **cross score** says how much the card at one end of a link disagrees with
the card at the other. It is computed for every link between two cards that
carry claims, and it is several numbers and not one, because "disagrees" means
several things.

The numbers come from the query
[cross scores](../.hql/queries/cross-scores.hql), which runs on the current
query language.

## What is compared

Two cards can be compared where they speak on the same atom. Each card has a
**belief** in an atom it speaks on:

- its credence, when its claim asserts the atom;
- one minus its credence, when its claim denies it.

A card that denies `same-as(abstraction, summarization)` at a credence of 0.75
believes that atom at 0.25. Two cards that both deny it at 0.75 hold the same
belief and agree completely.

An atom only one of the two cards speaks on contributes nothing. Silence is
not a belief of one half, and it is not disagreement.

## The numbers

| Number | Range | What it measures |
|---|---|---|
| `shared` | 0 and up | The atoms both cards speak on. When it is 0 the next three numbers are absent, since nothing can be compared. |
| `disagreement` | 0 to 1 | The mean distance between the two beliefs in a shared atom. 0 is the same belief, 1 is opposite certainties. |
| `divergence` | 0 to 1 bit | The same comparison as information: the mean Jensen-Shannon divergence between the two beliefs, each read as a coin with that probability. |
| `conflicts` | 0 and up | Shared atoms that one card asserts and the other denies, whatever the credences. |
| `distance` | 0 to 1 | How little the two cards talk about the same things: one minus the overlap of the arguments of their atoms, the size of the intersection over the size of the union. |

They answer different questions and do not move together.

- `disagreement` and `divergence` are about degree. Two cards can assert the
  same atom and still be 0.24 apart when one calls it proved and the other
  argued.
- `conflicts` is about logic. It counts contradictions and ignores how firmly
  either side is held.
- `distance` is not about disagreement at all. It says whether the two cards
  are close enough in subject for disagreement to be possible. A link with a
  distance near 1 joins cards with almost nothing in common to disagree
  about.

`divergence` is small where `disagreement` is moderate, because it grows
slowly near agreement and fast near contradiction: beliefs of 0.99 and 0.75
are 0.24 apart and 0.11 bits apart, while beliefs of 1 and 0 are 1 apart and 1
bit apart. Use `disagreement` to rank and `divergence` to weigh.

## A case where two numbers part

The prediction card denies that compression is the same as intelligence, at
0.75. The compression and prediction card states the equivalence as a
conjecture, at 0.4. One asserts and one denies, so `conflicts` is 1: this is
the single contradicted atom of the vault. The beliefs are 0.25 and 0.4, so
`disagreement` is 0.15. The logic reports a contradiction where the
probabilities report two cards that nearly agree the claim is doubtful.
Neither number is wrong. They are measuring different things, which is the
reason to keep both.

## What the scores do not see

- **Claims without an atom.** A third of the claims are in natural language
  only, and no score reads them. The design that would is the future query
  `claim-contradictions.hql`, which compares claims by meaning.
- **Conflict between different atoms.** The scores compare two cards only on
  atoms that are the same string. `is-a(a, b)` asserted on one card and
  `same-as(a, b)` denied on another are not compared here. Rules of inference
  now connect such atoms: an atom derived from some claims can meet a denial
  stated by another. That is described in [derivation](derivation.hmd), and
  its results are not part of any cross score.
- **Conflict between links.** Two cards that each declare the other a part of
  itself contradict one another in their links and not in their claims. The
  query [two-way links](../.hql/queries/two-way-links.hql) finds those.
- **Whether a credence is right.** The numbers start as priors set by evidence
  grade. A score is exactly as informative as the credences under it.

A low score therefore means the cards do not contradict each other where
they can be compared. It does not mean they are consistent.
